From: buntOS contributors
Subject: [PATCH] media: mediatek: add a driver for the MT6897 sensor interface

Add a V4L2 subdevice driver for the MIPI CSI-2 receiver of the MT6897. It
supports CSI port 0 as one four-lane D-PHY port over both halves of the
port, routed through seninf1, mux1 and cam-mux 0, with RAW8, RAW10 and
RAW12 Bayer formats under 2.5 Gbit/s per lane. The driver creates the media
device, binds the sensor through the fwnode graph, and powers the block and
its two power domains only while it streams.

Once the video decoder has started the clock scaling part of the VCP
firmware (MMDVFS), the first register write into the CAM_MAIN power domain
ends in an asynchronous SError while that firmware is stopped. With the
coprocessor running, the same power-up works. MediaTek's driver wakes the
coprocessor before it powers its domains (mtk_mmdvfs_enable_vcp()), so this
driver holds the coprocessor while the domains are on. Its runtime PM
callbacks start the coprocessor, power the domains and enable the clocks in
that order, synchronously and without device links, because the driver core
also resumes the device whenever a child node is added. The PM core leaves
the domains alone in system sleep: genpd would otherwise power them up on
every resume, with the coprocessor stopped.

The register sequence follows MediaTek's isp7sp seninf driver for CSI PHY
3.0 in the OnePlus MT6897 kernel modules. The D-PHY receive timing is the
one the stock driver of the SC820CS sensor asks for. With the vendor
default, which leaves the HS trail handling off, every packet from that
sensor arrives with a CRC error. The termination trims come from the fuses
when their checksum holds.

No DMA engine takes the cam-mux output yet. For bring-up, debugfs has a
stream file that starts and stops the sensor and the receiver, and a status
file with the CSI-2 and mux counters.
---
